# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Valida backend — example environment file
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in local values:
#   Windows PowerShell:  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is git-ignored. `.env.example` is committed.
# Governed by 15_DEVOPS_DEPLOYMENT.md sections 49-58.
#
# SECURITY RULE (15_DEVOPS_DEPLOYMENT.md section 52)
# This file contains variable names and safe placeholders only.
# Never commit real passwords, tokens or connection credentials.
# Production secret values are supplied through the hosting provider's secret
# management, never through a committed file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Node runtime mode. Values: development | test | production
NODE_ENV=development

# Explicit Valida application environment. Values: local | review | production
APP_ENV=local

# Port the backend HTTP server listens on. Defaults to 4000 if unset.
# Must be 1-65535. 0 is rejected on purpose: it would silently bind a random
# port, which is useful in a test but wrong in a deployment.
PORT=4000

# Log level. Values: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace | silent
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORS — Milestone B1
#
# Comma-separated list of EXACT frontend origins allowed to call this API.
# Format: scheme://host[:port] — no trailing slash, no path.
#
# "*" is rejected by configuration validation: an allowlist is explicit by
# definition, and a wildcard would expose the API to every site.
#
# Optional when APP_ENV=local (the two local values below are used by default).
# REQUIRED when APP_ENV=production — the backend refuses to start without it.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:4173

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Proxy trust — Milestone B1
#
# "false" (default) or the number of trusted proxy hops in front of this app.
#
# Do not set this to a number unless a proxy really is in front of the backend.
# Trusting X-Forwarded-For when nothing sets it lets any client spoof its IP and
# bypass the rate limit entirely. The correct production value depends on the
# actual hosting topology and must be confirmed per deployment.
TRUST_PROXY=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# General public API rate limit — Milestone B1
#
# Canonical starting policy: 120 requests per 60 seconds per client IP.
# The health endpoints are exempt so platform probes cannot exhaust the budget.
# The stricter Apply-specific limit arrives with the Apply endpoint in B5.
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=120

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MongoDB — Milestone B2
#
# REQUIRED. The backend connects at startup and refuses to start without a
# reachable database. Any connection string the MongoDB driver accepts:
#   mongodb://host[:port]/database
#   mongodb://host1:27017,host2:27017/database?replicaSet=rs0
#   mongodb+srv://cluster.example.net/database
# with no whitespace.
#
# The value below is a credential-free LOCAL placeholder. If your server needs
# a username and password, put them only in your own git-ignored `.env`, never
# here. The connection string is never logged or printed by the backend.
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/valida_dev

# How long startup waits for MongoDB before failing (1000-120000 ms).
MONGODB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Milestone B3 — upper bound on one public Jobs read (500-60000 ms). Also sent
# to MongoDB as maxTimeMS. A slower or unresponsive database makes the Jobs API
# answer 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE instead of hanging or returning an empty list.
MONGODB_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Real-database tests (`npm run test:db`, `npm run verify:b2`, `verify:b3`,
# `verify:b4`) read MONGODB_TEST_URI, not MONGODB_URI. Those tests delete records and drop
# indexes, so they refuse to run unless its database is named exactly
# `valida_test` or `valida_test_<suffix>`, differs from MONGODB_URI (here and in
# the shell), and the shell is not a production environment. Set it in the
# shell for the verification run rather than in `.env`, for example in
# PowerShell:
#   $env:MONGODB_TEST_URI = "mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/valida_test"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private resume storage — Milestone B4
#
# OPTIONAL. Left unset (as below), the backend starts and serves the Jobs API,
# and reports resume storage as not ready: /health/ready answers 503 and no
# Application could be accepted. That is the ONLY valid setting for a review or
# production deployment until the production storage provider is selected at
# deployment (Milestone C6, AWAITING INPUT).
#
# For LOCAL DEVELOPMENT you may enable the development-only local driver, which
# writes resume bytes to a private directory on this machine. It is refused at
# startup unless APP_ENV=local and NODE_ENV is not production.
#
# RESUME_STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT must be an ABSOLUTE path that is:
#   - outside this repository (and must not contain it);
#   - not inside a public/static/web-served directory (public, public_html,
#     static, www, wwwroot, htdocs, dist) and not C:\Users\Public;
#   - checked where it REALLY leads: symbolic links and Windows junctions in
#     the path are followed, and the two rules above apply to the result;
#   - not a drive or filesystem root.
# On Windows, choose a folder inside your own profile so its permissions are
# private to you, for example:
#   RESUME_STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=C:\Users\<you>\AppData\Local\valida-dev-private-resumes
# On Linux/macOS the directory must not be accessible to other users (mode 700);
# the backend creates it that way when it does not exist.
#
# Use synthetic test files only — never real candidate resumes in development.
# Tests never use these values: `npm run verify:b4` clears them for every run
# and uses its own temporary storage roots.
#
#   RESUME_STORAGE_DRIVER=local
#   RESUME_STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT YET USED — added by the milestone that implements them.
# These names are recorded here for visibility only and are intentionally
# absent from the variable list above until their milestone lands:
#
#   PUBLIC_SITE_URL          -> Release C (absolute URL generation)
#   TRANSACTIONAL_EMAIL_*    -> Milestone B6 (notification provider)
#   (production resume storage provider variables -> Milestone C6)
#
# Do not add values for these yet. Adding an unread variable creates the false
# impression that the corresponding capability exists.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
